`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- `STRICT_FILES` in `tests/docs/test_banned_stale_references.py` is a hand-curated allow-list, so any file the plan doesn't explicitly enroll gets zero guard coverage — "guard still passes" is not the same as "new contract is guarded."
- `DATA_PLACEMENT.md` is operational precisely because it exposes two numeric thresholds (10 MB, 1000 files). A contract that carries forward only the abstract "bulk-artifact-store" intent without those numbers loses testable precision.
- A TDD test that asserts a plan *documents* test-first sequencing does not verify the worker actually wrote the test first — self-referential prose checks are theatre unless paired with an external artefact (e.g., a failing-run log commit) or commit-ordering evidence.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

Verdict: MAJOR

Findings:
- [MAJOR] Stale-reference guard does not actually cover the new contract or checklist — `tests/docs/test_banned_stale_references.py:7-22` is a hard-coded `STRICT_FILES` list; no `docs/standards/*` path is in it. The plan's only stale-reference acceptance criterion (line 284) is "existing curated-doc stale-reference guard still passes", which is trivially true no matter what the new docs contain. Neither `docs/standards/TIER1_INDEXING_AND_CODE_PLACEMENT_CONTRACT.md` nor `docs/standards/TIER1_INDEXING_CHECKLIST.md` is added to `STRICT_FILES`, and the TDD test list (lines 240-264) contains no test that invokes `scan_stale_reference_hits` on the new contract/checklist. Net effect: the contract's own "legacy retirement rule" is enforced only by prose, and future drift into the new docs will not fail CI. This contradicts Resource Intelligence bullet at line 15 ("#2460 can follow the same style"). Fix: enroll both new files in `STRICT_FILES` as part of the plan (with allowlist handling for retirement-discussion quotes), and add a TDD test that asserts enrollment.
- [MAJOR] Threshold carry-forward from `DATA_PLACEMENT.md` is lost. `docs/standards/DATA_PLACEMENT.md:9-15` ties the placement rule to two concrete thresholds ("< 10 MB", "< 1000 files", "If a directory will exceed either threshold (10 MB or 1000 files), it belongs on the ace drive"). The plan's Deliverable (line 151) and pseudocode (lines 182-185) replace this with "bulk-artifact-store means a non-repo storage target for large, generated, or fast-growing artifacts" and no numbers at all — a grep of the plan for `10 MB`, `1000 files`, or `threshold` returns zero hits. TDD test `test_tier1_contract_requires_repo_vs_bulk_artifact_store_rule` (line 250) only requires "universal rule, definition, and example language present" — a contract that says "large artifacts live outside the repo" with no numeric threshold passes this test. This abstracts away the single operational part of the source standard, so workers in sibling repos cannot deterministically apply it. Fix: require the contract to either (a) cite `DATA_PLACEMENT.md` as the binding threshold source, or (b) replicate the 10 MB / 1000 files numbers; add a TDD assertion for whichever is chosen.
- [MINOR] Checklist status-field set is narrower than the contract's own required surfaces. Lines 192-194 / acceptance criterion line 279 require per-repo status for `operator_map_status`, `registry_status`, `data_placement_status` only. The contract itself (lines 270-271) requires seven independent trusted surfaces including `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `docs/README.md`, code/tests/docs routing tables, and source-hygiene rules. So a repo can score "missing" on AGENTS.md and pass the checklist because the checklist never records that dimension. Fix: align checklist status fields with the full contract surface list (or explicitly justify why only three are tracked).
- [MINOR] `#2465` same-section constraint is asserted in pseudocode (line 179 "contract must name #2465 in the same freshness section") but the matching TDD test `test_tier1_contract_marks_2465_as_daily_freshness_followthrough` (line 254) only checks that `#2465` appears anywhere in the contract. A contract that puts `#2465` in an unrelated "Implementation follow-through" section passes the test but violates the pseudocode. Fix: strengthen the test to assert co-location (e.g., `#2465` appears within N lines of the `daily freshness review` phrase).
- [MINOR] TDD sequencing assertion is self-referential. `test_tdd_sequence_requires_tests_fail_before_doc_edits` (line 261) scans plan text for a test-first sentence — it does not verify that `tests/docs/test_tier1_indexing_contract.py` was actually introduced by a commit preceding edits to the two new standards docs. Any worker can satisfy the assertion while writing the contract first and the tests afterward. Fix: either rely on commit-ordering evidence at verification time, require a captured failing-run log committed under `docs/reports/`, or drop the assertion as low-value.
- [MINOR] No required cross-link from the new contract to `DATA_PLACEMENT.md` or `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`. The plan treats these as upstream sources (line 37) but never requires the new contract to link back to them, so the new contract can silently fork their rules over time. Fix: add a TDD assertion that the contract explicitly references both source paths.

Checks performed:
- Read plan in full, cross-checked acceptance criteria against TDD Test List against pseudocode for coverage parity.
- Read `tests/docs/test_banned_stale_references.py` and `tests/helpers/stale_reference_docs.py` to confirm `STRICT_FILES` is a hand-curated allow-list and not a glob — new files do not auto-enroll.
- Grepped the plan for `10 MB`, `1000 files`, `threshold` (zero hits) to verify the DATA_PLACEMENT numeric thresholds were dropped, then read `docs/standards/DATA_PLACEMENT.md:9-15` to confirm those thresholds are the operational part of the source rule.
- Verified `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md:16-22` does list exactly the four tier-1 repos claimed at plan line 34 — BUSINESS_BRAIN scope claim is correct.
- Confirmed existence of `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`, `docs/standards/FILE_STRUCTURE_TAXONOMY.md`, `docs/reports/tier-1-indexing-freshness-latest.md` (all `EXISTS`).
- Compared checklist status-field set (three) against contract's required-surface set (seven) and flagged the mismatch.
- Compared pseudocode freshness-section co-location requirement (line 179) against matching TDD test (line 254) and found weaker enforcement.
